<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aeneid</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">29–19 BCE</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Aeneid is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> epic poem in twelve books by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virgil">Virgil</a>, written between about 29 and 19 BCE. It tells how the Trojan hero <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aeneas">Aeneas</a> escaped the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/troy">Troy</a> and travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, where his descendants would found <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, and it served as a national epic for the Augustan regime. It was the most studied Latin poem of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gavin-douglas">Gavin Douglas</a> translated it into Scots in 1513.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aeneid.jpg" alt="Aeneid" width="320" height="222" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Federico Barocci · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aeneas%27_Flight_from_Troy_by_Federico_Barocci.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
